nodecode-nvidia-app is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-nvidia:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-nvidia
  commit de75692b479dc88b95e428a9cb25091d3fb0e327 (nvidia-app)
  MIT, Copyright (c) Nous Research (declared in nvidia-app/plugin.json, which names the
  author there; the repository carries no LICENSE file of its own)

Rewritten: the plugin's one integration — NVIDIA App's local MCP server on loopback, published
at an address and token the app rewrites in its McpServer server.json — as an MCP client of the
cell's own over Nodecode's HTTP: the initialize handshake with its notification and session, the
Streamable HTTP body (a JSON answer or an event stream's last data: line), re-reading the
published address and token on every connection, and a session the app forgot handed once more
under a call. The plugin's twelve tools are registered under their original names, with their
arguments translated to the app's own, and its skill — the restricted Overlay workflow, the
explicit-state and no-verification-read rules, the exact game names, the error codes, and the
"report only what came back" discipline — is the cell's manual topic, /nvidia and its primer.

Left out: the plugin.json manifest, the MCP server declaration with its liveness and version
block (the cell is the client, so there is no connector to declare and no catalog line to relay),
the skill's deferral to Hermes's deferred tool catalog, tool_search and tool_describe (the cell
adds nvapp_list_tools, which asks the server for its own list), and the first-run PowerShell
probe, which is guidance in the manual rather than a tool. Nothing of the app's code is bundled:
every byte goes to the server NVIDIA App itself runs.
